this weekend marked world mental health  day and it feels  particularly important this year as we  all face the worrying uncertainty that  coronavirus  brings aleister campbell tony blair's  former director of communications has  spoken out in the past about his own  personal experience and he's written a  book  on surviving depression he joins us now  and  you've been very open about your own  mental health challenges you've spoken  very eloquently about it before just  explain to people who might not been  familiar with  what happened about your own experience  well the book is about my mental health  story and also without my family so  my own psychosis and alcohol problems in  the 80s i had a brother who was  now dead sadly i schizophrenia another  brother who had addiction issues  a cousin who took his own life so i've  tried to kind of tell that story but in  the broader context  of what i think is a growing mental  health crisis which is going to be  exacerbated by some of the things that  you've been talking on  about earlier in the program so i've  really tried to  address my own mental health and i think  i'm now in a better shape than i've ever  been  because i've really gone into it deep  but also then  the second part of the book living  better is about actually trying to find  other ways that other people in  different parts of the world  are looking at depression i call it the  search for cure i'm not sure we'll ever  get a cure  but i do think there are things that we  can do for ourselves and for each other  that can help us to deal with depression  much better and it feels particularly  important  at the moment with the anxiety that we  all feel no matter  what our own experiences are with mental  health with a pandemic i mean it must be  a really really difficult time for so  many people who are  battling with their mental health well  it's interesting  mind and other mental health charities  have been very very clear that although  yes  everybody's struggling to a certain  degree with anxiety  those who have pre-existing mental  health conditions  are the ones who are struggling most and  i think it was really interesting  actually watching your  your program just now um in a sense the  whole story of covet i think  has been that the government has been  constantly trying to catch up  with decisions that it should have been  planning for  and i think maybe the royal college of  psychiatrists have said  that they think mental illness is going  to be the second pandemic  of covid we've seen a huge rise in  people reporting symptoms of depression  the suicide figures are going up and  we've got an economic  catastrophe coming down the track and we  know what happened the last time with  the global financial crisis  there is a direct link to suicide now if  you've got that if you know  that's coming it's the duty of  government to plan for that  and i don't have any sense of the  government planning for this  you hear other world leaders they talk  about mental health  people like jacinda ardern in new  zealand angela merkel cuomo in new york  they've been talking about the mental  health implications from the word go  and we just i don't think we're even the  government's even got this on its radar  it is concerning um there was research  by sky news that showed that over the  last six months there were  actually over 200 000 fewer referrals to  mental health services compared to last  year i was speaking  earlier this week to the chief executive  of saying the mental health charity  saying that  a lot of people are worried about  putting extra strain on the nhs  about coming forward with when they are  really suffering  from mental health issues are you  worried people aren't seeking out the  help they need  well look that is definitely happening  you talk to  and look i've been doing that myself i  mean i had a bit of a  kind of i had a manic spelling locked  down and i also had a very big dick  and you find yourself thinking oh well  i'll just kind of see it through because  and i think that's a natural reaction  because we're all conscious of the fact  that covid  is such a big problem for our health  services but i get the feeling now that  this actually if we're not  careful is going to become an even an  even bigger problem  because what seems to be happening is  yes the cases are going back up  the death rate seems to be fairly being  kept fairly low  fairly stable but i think these mental  health conditions they're storing up and  they're storing up and they're storing  up  and so i think it's it's been  interesting to uh  to listen to people in the health  service saying look if you are  kind of struggling if you are suffering  please do come forward  so i think that we we we've got to  understand that this is going to be the  next wave of this  and i really really worry the  government's not on the case i saw  you mentioned it was world mental health  day yesterday i watched boris johnson's  video about that  i mean there was nothing in it apart  from a few cliches and platitudes and a  few references to william winston  churchill having had depression  this is going to be the next big  challenge for government and they've got  to start preparing for it  you mentioned there your own experiences  doing sort of lockdown during the  pandemic  um what would your advice be to people  watching the show maybe who  feel that they are struggling is there  any things that people can do just to  try  and make themselves feel better to try  and pull themselves out is there any  advice that you can  give people well it is hard and it's  very hard to sort of give general advice  to people who will be having their own  very very personal  struggles but i do think that talking is  the single most important thing that you  can do  i actually think that you know in in the  book my partner fiona has written a  chapter about what it's like  living with me when i'm depressed and it  was actually interesting for me to read  that  to get her sense we've obviously talked  about it for 40 years  but actually sometimes when you write  things down you get a better sense  and what's interesting from her  perspective was that she felt there was  nothing she could do  and yet for me as the person who was  going through the depressions  you know pretty much every stage of the  40 years we've been together  actually her just being there for me to  talk to when i felt like talking was  incredibly important  so the most important thing to me is  actually to  not to feel ashamed never to feel that  it's anybody's fault  it just is depression anxiety these  conditions  just are but i often feel now the fact  that whereas i used to be in denial  about it i used to not talk about it  now that i'm very open about it the  first thing i do when i feel it coming  on is say to fiona  it's happening again we both worked out  ways to deal with that better so i'd say  talking is the single most important  thing that we can do  whether with a family member a friend a  colleague or  it may be with a medical professional  but just talk  is the best way out of it thank you it's  really good advice there and obviously  very important for people  like yourself with a public profile  people have been very successful in  their lives  to talk about your own experiences so we  really thank you for coming the program  to doing that thank you
 

